# Matched documentary comparison and author review

HIT-SOLO-002, 7 October 2026. These are Codex's proposed interpretations of the [frozen six-document packet](plan.md). Both methods use the same sources and six common questions. The baseline was written with knowledge of the HIT draft. Neither it nor the comparison is an independent rating, a blind trial or a measure of effort. The scientific judgments remain pending.

## Capable structured review

| Common question | Baseline conclusion and evidence chain | Remaining uncertainty and proportionate request |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Which authority acted? | The named regulatory actor is Ofqual's Board. Its announcement, reported requirement to exam boards and subsequent issue reports form the direction-to-execution chain. See EC-BOARD, EC-DECISION, EC-REISSUE-REQUIREMENT and EC-ISSUE-COMMAND in the draft. | Request the original operational instruction and implementation reconciliation if the decision requires confirmation beyond public reporting. Do not attribute each board's code operation to the regulator. |
| 2. What access and reasoning preceded the decision? | EC-ADVICE identifies a seasonal advice route. EC-REASONS supplies public reasons that differ from ratifying a model output. | The packet does not resolve the specific underlying evidence accessed before the event. Public reasons may be selective; request the event's evidence bundle and deliberation record. |
| 3. What reconsideration and change took effect? | EC-RECONSIDERATION, EC-REPLACEMENT, EC-IMPLEMENTATION, EC-ISSUE-CORRECTION and EC-ISSUE-REFORM support distinct propositions about a bounded systemic reversal and reported use of a different award rule. | EC-APPEALS-LIMIT prevents extension to universally accessible individual appeals. No conclusion follows about the usual process, each technical configuration or earlier control. |
| 4. What harm and remedy are established? | EC-HARM records formal acknowledgement. EC-REMEDY-DIRECTION and EC-ISSUE-REPAIR support a directed record-correction response. | This does not establish full repair for all harmed people. EC-EXTERNAL-LIMIT and EC-ADMISSIONS-LIMIT retain excluded recipients and unresolved opportunities. Request recipient-linked reconciliation before making a universal-remedy claim. |
| 5. What could change the conclusion? | The packet is dominated by involved institutions' reporting. The event and follow-up differ from the sources' later publication dates. The public code is not a production log. | Independently checked execution records, internal deliberation or contrary recipient-level evidence could change the interpretations. No such material was obtained here. |
| 6. What follows? | Preserve the distinction between a supported bounded intervention and unestablished broader effectiveness. The presently supported response is a scoped report with explicit requests where the user's decision needs stronger assurance. | No recommendation to reopen all grades, certify the institution or change its current operation follows from this historical assessment. Additional action depends on the actual institutional decision and available authority. |

## HIT comparison

| HIT item | Draft proposal | Relation to the capable baseline |
|---|---|---|
| Counsel | IE, with event-specific access unresolved | Proposed tie on the uncertainty and next evidence request. A formal-route interpretation of 1 remains for adjudication. |
| Judgment | 2, based on the event's documented public reasoning | Proposed tie on the bounded reasoning evidence; the score compresses an interpretation that still needs review. |
| Command | 2, exercised regulatory direction with reported consequence | Proposed tie on actor, mechanism and consequence. |
| Correction | 2, exceptional systemic reconsideration | Proposed tie. Both preserve the limits of individual appeal routes. |
| Repair | 2, proposed operational-direction route | Proposed tie on the limited remedy claim. Neither method establishes that every harm was repaired. |
| Reform | 2, identified rule change with reported implementation | Proposed tie on the changed architecture and temporal boundary. |
| Integrity | Limited for each component and overall | Proposed tie on source dependence and missing internal provenance. HIT provides a fixed derivation rule. |

The current contribution is a complete inspectable representation of these proposed judgments with executable contract checks. No unique substantive advantage, reduction in error or effort, or practical adoption is demonstrated. Shared authorship and matching encourage agreement. These proposed ties are not an empirical equivalence result.

## Author decision bundle

The author should review the source passages and the complete draft together, then record acceptance, revision, rejection or unresolved status with reasons. Approval to continue, a passing validator or merging the files is not that decision.

| Item | Proposed interpretation | Live alternative or objection |
|---|---|---|
| SOLO-002-AD1 | The exploratory event boundary and automation-scope application are usable for a bounded development example. | An exceptional regulatory response may be too remote from ordinary case-level oversight for the intended claim. Keep the example labelled or narrow its use. |
| SOLO-002-AD2 | Counsel IE preserves missing event-specific access evidence. | Counsel 1 may fit if the seasonal technical-advice route establishes formal presence for the event. The record must not invent a stricter evidence rule to avoid that possibility. |
| SOLO-002-AD3 | The Chair's stated reasons and reversal support event-level Judgment 2. | Public reasons may not sufficiently establish independent evaluation. IE remains plausible if the account is too indirect or selective; ceremonial 1 would require its own affirmative basis. |
| SOLO-002-AD4 | Command, Correction and Reform 2 apply to the bounded regulatory act and reported follow-through. | Inspect the direction relationship, source dependence and event timing. Do not transfer these findings to all exam boards, teachers, candidates or the full year. |
| SOLO-002-AD5 | Repair 2 uses specification 6.5's operationally directed record-correction route after formal acknowledgement of harm. | Repair 1 may be appropriate if the link between acknowledged harm and the directed remedy is insufficiently specific. Actual individual receipt is not an invented universal requirement when the specification permits operational direction. Universal repair remains unsupported under either reading. |
| SOLO-002-AD6 | The capable-baseline outcomes are proposed ties, with remaining scientific limits visible. | The author may identify a missed distinction or a loss. No comparison outcome is fixed by the desire to show usefulness. |

Substantive disagreement can reveal a packet limitation or a governing-rule ambiguity. Record which one is at issue. A rule change would require the repository's contract governance; this draft does not amend the rubric. All items currently await author adjudication.
